import sysl.time.*

// Wall clocks, timelines, and the conversion between them.
//
// The axis this owns is **a conversion that can succeed twice**. Every other program in the set
// converts things that either work or do not, and `Option` and `Result` are the two shapes that
// covers. A wall clock reading turned into a moment is neither: it usually has one answer, in one
// hour of the year it has none, and in one other hour it has two — and both of the two are correct,
// for different questions. Nothing in the set had asked for that before.
//
// The program is a recurring-meeting expander, because that is where the distinction cannot be
// avoided. A meeting is a rule about a wall clock — every Tuesday at half past nine, in New York —
// and the answer a participant elsewhere needs is a point on the timeline. Expanding the rule puts
// the conversion in the middle of the program rather than in a corner of it.
//
// **The assertion the whole thing is for**: across a daylight-saving change, "next week's meeting"
// and "one hundred and sixty-eight hours from now" are different moments, and *both are right*.
// One is 167 hours later and reads as half past nine; the other is 168 hours later and reads as
// half past ten. A single type for both could only ever have been wrong about one of them.
//
// Every expected value came from outside: the transitions, the offsets and each meeting time were
// computed with Python's `zoneinfo` against the real tz database. That was worth doing for its own
// sake — Python subtracts two aware date-times in one zone by *wall clock*, so the first attempt at
// the reference values reported the week across the spring change as 168 hours. The confusion this
// program is about is not a hypothetical one.
//
// What it found, in the order it bit:
//
//   * **Two thirds of this program became a library module, and what is left is the third that is
//     actually about zones.** It was written as five files: the two quantities, the civil calendar,
//     the zone table, the meeting, and these checks. The first two were a date-time library wearing
//     a guide's clothes — nothing in them is about *this* problem, and every program that ever
//     wanted a date would have had to write them again. They are `sysl.time` now, imported at the
//     top of each file here, and the three that remain are the ones a tz database does not answer
//     for you. The rule this followed is the one `guide/fft` followed when `sysl.math` landed: a
//     guide that hand-rolls what the library supplies is teaching a workaround.
//   * **A program cannot render a type the library owns, and moving a type into the library is
//     therefore a decision about who may print it.** `zone.sysl` used to carry an
//     `impl Display for Instant`. Once `Instant` moved, that block had no home: coherence
//     (`02 § Coherence`) wants a type of the writing module named in the block, and `Display` and
//     `Instant` are both the library's. This is the orphan rule doing its job rather than a defect —
//     two programs each rendering `Instant` their own way is exactly what it prevents — but the
//     consequence is worth stating, because it is one-way. A type that ships must arrive with the
//     renderings it will ever have. `sysl.time` renders one now, in the same words this file
//     checked before.
//   * **The difference of two moments could not be spelled `-` — answered.** Every operator row was
//     `op(self, rhs: Rhs) -> Self`, so an implementation said what it *took* and never what it
//     *produced*. `Instant + Duration`, `Instant - Duration` and `Duration ± Duration` all wrote
//     themselves; `Instant - Instant -> Duration` was refused, because `Sub`'s result was fixed to
//     the type on the left. Three quarters of an algebra was spellable and the quarter that was not
//     was the operation the library exists to provide, so it was a named function called `since`.
//
//     An operator trait carries its result as `Out` now, as well as its operand as `Rhs` (`14 §7`),
//     and that is the same change `guide/matrix` was written for: a vector space needs `Vector *
//     Vector -> real` and a timeline needs `Instant - Instant -> Duration`, which are one feature
//     asked for by two problems. `sysl.time` writes both rows of `Sub` on `Instant`, told apart by
//     the type of the right operand and nothing else, so the checks below subtract two instants
//     with `-` like any other arithmetic. `since` stays as the named spelling, because
//     `later - earlier` is right and `earlier - later` is just as easy to write.
//
//     **The finding moved into the library with the types and was answered there**, which is the
//     part worth keeping: a gap that becomes a shipped module's gap is fixed once for everybody
//     instead of worked around once per program that wants a date.
//   * **A derived scalar and a one-field struct are exactly complementary, and neither is what a
//     quantity wants.** `type Instant = new i64` inherits its base's whole catalog for free — `==`,
//     `<`, `+`, `str` — and may replace none of it, so it arrives with `Instant + Instant`, which
//     is nonsense, and cannot be given `Instant + Duration`, which is not. A struct of one field
//     gets the algebra exactly right and arrives with nothing at all: five `impl` blocks per type
//     before anything can be compared or printed. So a quantity type chooses between the catalog
//     and the meaning. What changed is who pays: the price is now paid once, in the library, by
//     everybody rather than by each program that wants a date.
//   * **The fixed-offset half of the conversion was never this program's, and is now the
//     library's.** `to_local` and `resolve` each hand-rolled the same two lines of offset
//     arithmetic, and the sign of it is the thing everybody gets backwards once. `at_offset` and
//     `from_offset` are in `sysl.time` now, both **total** — which is precisely what marks the line
//     between them and a zone, since a zone answers the second question with none or two. What is
//     left in `resolve` is the part that is genuinely about a rule with a history, and the
//     non-moving case is now one call to the library rather than arithmetic repeated a third time.
//     It caught something too: the transition tables claimed to compute their instants from the
//     published rule and in fact held the UTC hours 7 and 6 as literals — the arithmetic already
//     done, by hand, with the paragraph above it saying it had not been. They go through
//     `from_offset` from a 02:00 local reading now, which is what the rule actually says.
//   * **The three-way answer needed nothing new**, and that is a result rather than an absence. A
//     data enum already spells "one answer, or none, or two, and here they are"; `Resolution` is an
//     ordinary enum and the compiler's coverage checking is what makes a caller handle all three.
//     The axis the problem was chosen for turns out to be covered by the machinery that was already
//     there — which is the useful thing to know before designing a library around it.
//   * **An enum cannot render its own variant names — and the three tables this reported turn out
//     to be three different asks.** A simple enum's name answers `T::Image(v)` with the word the
//     variant is spelled with (`09 §2`), so the name-only case never needed a table: the library's
//     `weekday_name` is that case and is now one attribute read, which is the one of the three the
//     move behind an import actually fixed.
//
//     Neither of this program's own two is that case, and saying why is worth more than the
//     original complaint. `ZoneId` maps `NewYork` to `"America/New_York"` — a tz identifier, which
//     is a fact about the database and not about the declaration, so nothing but a table can hold
//     it. `Resolution` is a **data** enum, where `Image` is refused in as many words, because a
//     value there is a variant plus a payload and a name answers for half of it — and this
//     program's renderer builds its sentence out of the payload anyway.
//
//     What is left of the finding is the narrow thing: `str` on an enum is still refused, so a
//     value that is nothing but its name is printed by asking for the name rather than by rendering
//     the value.
//   * **A `val` could not be sliced**, third program to report that, and the report was acted on:
//     a view of read-only storage is a `[]const T`, which carries the property rather than losing
//     it. The two transition tables are still reached through a function whose whole body is an
//     index, which is now a choice rather than the only way to reach them.
//   * **A built-in is on nobody's left.** `Duration * 2` is writable and `2 * Duration` is not, so
//     the scaling in a date-time library only ever reads one way round.
//
// Deliberately not modelled, so that what is left stays a guide problem rather than a second
// standard library: the tz database, leap seconds, calendars other than the proleptic Gregorian,
// and any zone rule more elaborate than one yearly pair of transitions.

check(name: string, got: string, want: string)
    if got == want then print(s"ok    $name = $got")
    else print(s"FAIL  $name = $got, wanted $want")
end check

print("-- instants and durations")
var t = Instant(1000000)
check("an instant moved by a duration", str((t + 2.hours).us), "7201000000")
check("and moved back", str((t + 2.hours - 2.hours).us), "1000000")
// Both rows of `Sub` on one type, in two lines: the right operand is a `Duration` above and an
// `Instant` here, and that is the whole of what selects between landing on the timeline and
// measuring across it.
check("the difference of two instants", str(whole_hours(t + 3.hours - t)), "3")
check("and the named spelling agrees", str(whole_hours(since(t + 3.hours, t))), "3")
check("an offset renders", offset_text(Offset(330)), "+05:30")
check("a negative one", offset_text(Offset(-300)), "-05:00")
check("and zero is not an offset at all", offset_text(Offset(0)), "Z")

print("-- days and civil dates")
check("the epoch", str(date_at(1970, 1, 1).day), "0")
check("the day before it", str(date_at(1969, 12, 31).day), "-1")
check("a leap day", str(date_at(2000, 2, 29).day), "11016")
check("a date this year", str(date_at(2026, 7, 28).day), "20662")
check("and one long ago", str(date_at(1900, 3, 1).day), "-25508")
check("a day number renders as a date", date_text(LocalDate(20520)), "2026-03-08")
check("and before the epoch", date_text(LocalDate(-1)), "1969-12-31")
check("1900 was not a leap year", str(date_at(1900, 3, 1).day - date_at(1900, 2, 28).day), "1")
check("2000 was", str(date_at(2000, 3, 1).day - date_at(2000, 2, 28).day), "2")

print("-- the day of the week")
check("the epoch was a Thursday", weekday_name(weekday(LocalDate(0))), "Thursday")
check("the day before it", weekday_name(weekday(LocalDate(-1))), "Wednesday")
check("the second Sunday of March 2026", str(date_at(2026, 3, 8).day), "20520")
check("which was a Sunday", weekday_name(weekday(date_at(2026, 3, 8))), "Sunday")
check("and the third of March a Tuesday", weekday_name(weekday(date_at(2026, 3, 3))), "Tuesday")
check("the next Tuesday on or after a Tuesday is itself",
      date_text(on_or_after(date_at(2026, 3, 3), Tuesday)), "2026-03-03")
check("and after a Wednesday is six days later",
      date_text(on_or_after(date_at(2026, 3, 4), Tuesday)), "2026-03-10")

print("-- a time of day")
check("half past nine", time_text(time_at(9, 30, 0)), "09:30")
check("with seconds, when there are any", time_text(time_at(9, 30, 7)), "09:30:07")
check("midnight", time_text(time_at(0, 0, 0)), "00:00")
check("the last minute of the day", time_text(time_at(23, 59, 0)), "23:59")
check("a wall clock reading round-trips", datetime_text(wall_of(wall_us(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30, 0)))),
      "2026-03-08 02:30")
check("and one before the epoch", datetime_text(wall_of(wall_us(datetime_at(1969, 12, 31, 23, 59, 0)))),
      "1969-12-31 23:59")

// The transitions are the one part of a zone a reader can check against a calendar, so they are
// checked as calendar readings rather than as the microsecond counts the table actually holds.
// Every one of them was worked out from the published rule — the second Sunday of March at 02:00
// local for the United States, the last Sunday of March at 01:00 UTC for the European Union — and
// the two lists disagreeing about which day is the point.
print("-- when the clocks move")
check("New York, spring 2023", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 0)), "2023-03-12 07:00 Z")
check("New York, autumn 2023", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 1)), "2023-11-05 06:00 Z")
check("New York, spring 2024", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 2)), "2024-03-10 07:00 Z")
check("New York, autumn 2024", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 3)), "2024-11-03 06:00 Z")
check("New York, spring 2025", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 4)), "2025-03-09 07:00 Z")
check("New York, autumn 2025", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 5)), "2025-11-02 06:00 Z")
check("New York, spring 2026", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 6)), "2026-03-08 07:00 Z")
check("New York, autumn 2026", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 7)), "2026-11-01 06:00 Z")
check("New York, spring 2027", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 8)), "2027-03-14 07:00 Z")
check("New York, autumn 2027", instant_text(transition(NewYork, 9)), "2027-11-07 06:00 Z")
check("Berlin, spring 2023", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 0)), "2023-03-26 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, autumn 2023", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 1)), "2023-10-29 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, spring 2024", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 2)), "2024-03-31 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, autumn 2024", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 3)), "2024-10-27 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, spring 2025", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 4)), "2025-03-30 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, autumn 2025", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 5)), "2025-10-26 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, spring 2026", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 6)), "2026-03-29 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, autumn 2026", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 7)), "2026-10-25 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, spring 2027", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 8)), "2027-03-28 01:00 Z")
check("Berlin, autumn 2027", instant_text(transition(Berlin, 9)), "2027-10-31 01:00 Z")
check("in 2026 the two zones are twenty days apart",
      str((transition(Berlin, 6).us - transition(NewYork, 6).us) / us_per_day), "20")

print("-- an instant seen from a wall")
var noon = Instant(1772548200000000i64)
check("in UTC", zoned_text(to_local(noon, Utc)), "2026-03-03 14:30 Z")
check("in New York, before the change", zoned_text(to_local(noon, NewYork)), "2026-03-03 09:30 -05:00")
check("in Berlin", zoned_text(to_local(noon, Berlin)), "2026-03-03 15:30 +01:00")
check("in Kolkata, at half past the hour", zoned_text(to_local(noon, Kolkata)), "2026-03-03 20:00 +05:30")
var later = Instant(1773149400000000i64)
check("a week later in UTC", zoned_text(to_local(later, Utc)), "2026-03-10 13:30 Z")
check("in New York, after the change", zoned_text(to_local(later, NewYork)), "2026-03-10 09:30 -04:00")
check("in Berlin, which has not changed yet", zoned_text(to_local(later, Berlin)), "2026-03-10 14:30 +01:00")
check("and in Kolkata, which never does", zoned_text(to_local(later, Kolkata)), "2026-03-10 19:00 +05:30")

print("-- the offset in force")
check("New York in January", str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(1772548200000000i64)).minutes), "-300")
check("New York in July", str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(1773149400000000i64)).minutes), "-240")
check("one microsecond before the spring jump",
      str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(1772953200000000i64 - 1i64)).minutes), "-300")
check("and at the jump itself", str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(1772953200000000i64)).minutes), "-240")
check("one microsecond before the autumn jump",
      str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(1793512800000000i64 - 1i64)).minutes), "-240")
check("and at that jump", str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(1793512800000000i64)).minutes), "-300")
check("UTC never moves", str(offset_at(Utc, Instant(1773149400000000i64)).minutes), "0")
check("nor does Kolkata", str(offset_at(Kolkata, Instant(1773149400000000i64)).minutes), "330")

// The three answers a wall clock reading can have. Every date-time library in wide use gets this
// interface wrong in its own way — one returns a single instant and silently picks, one throws for
// the hole and picks for the overlap, one takes a flag saying which of two you meant — and all
// three are shapes an enum makes unnecessary.
print("-- a reading that happens once")
describe(r: Resolution) -> string = r match
    Once(at) -> f"once at ${instant_text(at)}%s"
    Skipped(gap, at, shifted) -> f"skipped, ${whole_hours(gap)}%dh, clock jumped ${instant_text(at)}%s"
    Twice(earlier, later) -> f"twice, ${instant_text(earlier)}%s and ${instant_text(later)}%s"

check("an ordinary winter morning in New York",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 3, 9, 30, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-03-03 14:30 Z")
check("an ordinary summer morning",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 10, 9, 30, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-03-10 13:30 Z")
check("a zone that never moves",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 10, 9, 30, 0), Kolkata)), "once at 2026-03-10 04:00 Z")
check("UTC, where the question is not a question",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 10, 9, 30, 0), Utc)), "once at 2026-03-10 09:30 Z")
check("the last reading before the spring hole",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 1, 59, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-03-08 06:59 Z")
check("and the first one after it",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 3, 0, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-03-08 07:00 Z")

print("-- a reading that never happens")
check("half past two on the eighth of March, in New York",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30, 0), NewYork)),
      "skipped, 1h, clock jumped 2026-03-08 07:00 Z")
check("as is every reading in that hour",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 0, 0), NewYork)),
      "skipped, 1h, clock jumped 2026-03-08 07:00 Z")
check("up to its last minute",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 59, 0), NewYork)),
      "skipped, 1h, clock jumped 2026-03-08 07:00 Z")
check("half past two on the twenty-ninth of March, in Berlin",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 29, 2, 30, 0), Berlin)),
      "skipped, 1h, clock jumped 2026-03-29 01:00 Z")
check("the same day is unremarkable in New York",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 29, 2, 30, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-03-29 06:30 Z")
check("and the eighth of March unremarkable in Berlin",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30, 0), Berlin)), "once at 2026-03-08 01:30 Z")

print("-- a reading that happens twice")
check("half past one on the first of November, in New York",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 11, 1, 1, 30, 0), NewYork)),
      "twice, 2026-11-01 05:30 Z and 2026-11-01 06:30 Z")
check("the two are an hour apart",
      str(whole_hours(since(Instant(1793514600000000i64), Instant(1793511000000000i64)))), "1")
check("one o'clock exactly, also twice",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 11, 1, 1, 0, 0), NewYork)),
      "twice, 2026-11-01 05:00 Z and 2026-11-01 06:00 Z")
check("two o'clock, only once",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 11, 1, 2, 0, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-11-01 07:00 Z")
check("midnight that day, only once",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 11, 1, 0, 0, 0), NewYork)), "once at 2026-11-01 04:00 Z")
check("half past two on the twenty-fifth of October, in Berlin",
      describe(resolve(datetime_at(2026, 10, 25, 2, 30, 0), Berlin)),
      "twice, 2026-10-25 00:30 Z and 2026-10-25 01:30 Z")
check("a calendar takes the first of the two",
      instant_text(resolve_calendar(datetime_at(2026, 11, 1, 1, 30, 0), NewYork)), "2026-11-01 05:30 Z")
check("and pushes a skipped one forward by the gap",
      instant_text(resolve_calendar(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30, 0), NewYork)), "2026-03-08 07:30 Z")
check("which reads as half past three", zoned_text(to_local(resolve_calendar(datetime_at(2026, 3, 8, 2, 30, 0), NewYork), NewYork)),
      "2026-03-08 03:30 -04:00")

// The section the whole program exists for. Two ways of saying "next week", one instant apart, and
// **both of them right** — which is why neither can be the default and why the two operations have
// to be spelled differently. Adding a week of *timeline* to the meeting moves it an hour late on
// the wall; keeping the *reading* and asking again moves it 167 hours along the timeline.
print("-- next week, and one hundred and sixty-eight hours")
var tuesdays = Meeting(Tuesday, time_at(9, 30, 0), NewYork)
var mar3 = resolve_calendar(LocalDateTime(date_at(2026, 3, 3), time_at(9, 30, 0)), NewYork)
var by_calendar = next_week(tuesdays, date_at(2026, 3, 3))
var by_clock = mar3 + 168.hours

check("the meeting on the third of March", instant_text(mar3), "2026-03-03 14:30 Z")
check("next week's meeting, by the calendar", instant_text(by_calendar), "2026-03-10 13:30 Z")
check("which reads as the meeting time", zoned_text(to_local(by_calendar, NewYork)), "2026-03-10 09:30 -04:00")
check("one hundred and sixty-eight hours later, by the clock", instant_text(by_clock), "2026-03-10 14:30 Z")
check("which reads an hour late", zoned_text(to_local(by_clock, NewYork)), "2026-03-10 10:30 -04:00")
check("the two answers differ", str(by_calendar != by_clock), "true")
check("by exactly an hour", str(whole_hours(since(by_clock, by_calendar))), "1")
check("so a week of calendar was 167 hours of timeline", str(whole_hours(since(by_calendar, mar3))), "167")
check("in autumn the same week is 169", str(whole_hours(since(
      next_week(tuesdays, date_at(2026, 10, 27)),
      resolve_calendar(LocalDateTime(date_at(2026, 10, 27), time_at(9, 30, 0)), NewYork)))), "169")
check("in a week with no transition it is 168", str(whole_hours(since(
      next_week(tuesdays, date_at(2026, 3, 10)),
      resolve_calendar(LocalDateTime(date_at(2026, 3, 10), time_at(9, 30, 0)), NewYork)))), "168")
check("Berlin gets its own 167, three weeks later", str(whole_hours(since(
      next_week(Meeting(Tuesday, time_at(9, 30, 0), Berlin), date_at(2026, 3, 24)),
      resolve_calendar(LocalDateTime(date_at(2026, 3, 24), time_at(9, 30, 0)), Berlin)))), "167")
check("and Kolkata always 168", str(whole_hours(since(
      next_week(Meeting(Tuesday, time_at(9, 30, 0), Kolkata), date_at(2026, 3, 3)),
      resolve_calendar(LocalDateTime(date_at(2026, 3, 3), time_at(9, 30, 0)), Kolkata)))), "168")

// Six weeks of one rule, read from two walls. The organiser's column never changes, which is what
// the rule promised; the participant's column moves twice, which is what the rule cost them.
print("-- six weeks of one meeting")
var dates: []Instant = [Instant(0); 6]
var n = expand(tuesdays, date_at(2026, 2, 24), dates)

check("six were asked for and six came back", str(n), "6")
check("week 1", agenda_line(dates[0], NewYork, Berlin),
      "2026-02-24 09:30 -05:00 / 2026-02-24 15:30 +01:00")
check("week 2", agenda_line(dates[1], NewYork, Berlin),
      "2026-03-03 09:30 -05:00 / 2026-03-03 15:30 +01:00")
check("week 3, New York has moved", agenda_line(dates[2], NewYork, Berlin),
      "2026-03-10 09:30 -04:00 / 2026-03-10 14:30 +01:00")
check("week 4", agenda_line(dates[3], NewYork, Berlin),
      "2026-03-17 09:30 -04:00 / 2026-03-17 14:30 +01:00")
check("week 5", agenda_line(dates[4], NewYork, Berlin),
      "2026-03-24 09:30 -04:00 / 2026-03-24 14:30 +01:00")
check("week 6, Berlin catches up", agenda_line(dates[5], NewYork, Berlin),
      "2026-03-31 09:30 -04:00 / 2026-03-31 15:30 +02:00")
check("the first two are a plain week apart", str(whole_hours(since(dates[1], dates[0]))), "168")
check("the week that crosses the change is not", str(whole_hours(since(dates[2], dates[1]))), "167")
check("and the rest are", str(whole_hours(since(dates[5], dates[4]))), "168")
check("the meeting never moved on the organiser's wall",
      time_text(to_local(dates[0], NewYork).local.time) + " " + time_text(to_local(dates[5], NewYork).local.time),
      "09:30 09:30")
check("and moved twice on the participant's",
      time_text(to_local(dates[1], Berlin).local.time) + " " + time_text(to_local(dates[2], Berlin).local.time)
        + " " + time_text(to_local(dates[5], Berlin).local.time),
      "15:30 14:30 15:30")

print("-- who is awake")
check("Berlin is at work for week 1", str(free_at(dates[0], Berlin, time_at(9, 0, 0), time_at(17, 0, 0))), "true")
check("and for week 3", str(free_at(dates[2], Berlin, time_at(9, 0, 0), time_at(17, 0, 0))), "true")
check("Kolkata is not, at eight in the evening",
      str(free_at(dates[0], Kolkata, time_at(9, 0, 0), time_at(17, 0, 0))), "false")
check("nor at seven", str(free_at(dates[2], Kolkata, time_at(9, 0, 0), time_at(17, 0, 0))), "false")
check("New York is, being where the meeting was set",
      str(free_at(dates[0], NewYork, time_at(9, 0, 0), time_at(17, 0, 0))), "true")
check("a day that opens at the meeting is open", str(free_at(dates[0], NewYork, time_at(9, 30, 0), time_at(17, 0, 0))), "true")
check("and one that closes at it is not", str(free_at(dates[0], NewYork, time_at(9, 0, 0), time_at(9, 30, 0))), "false")

// The two directions checked against each other over a whole year rather than at the handful of
// points a person would think to try. Reading an instant on a wall and then asking what instant
// that reading was has to give the instant back — **except** in the repeated hour, where the
// question has two answers and a calendar's rule is to take the first. One hour of the year fails
// to round-trip, and it is precisely the second pass through the overlap.
print("-- every hour of a year")
round_trips(z: ZoneId, from: LocalDate, hours_in: int) -> int
    var base = long(from.day) * us_per_day
    var missed = 0

    for i in 0..<hours_in
        var t = Instant(base + long(i) * us_per_hour)

        if resolve_calendar(to_local(t, z).local, z) != t then missed += 1

    missed
end round_trips

check("2026 has 8760 hours", str(date_at(2027, 1, 1).day - date_at(2026, 1, 1).day), "365")
check("UTC round-trips every one", str(round_trips(Utc, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 8760)), "0")
check("so does Kolkata", str(round_trips(Kolkata, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 8760)), "0")
check("New York misses exactly one", str(round_trips(NewYork, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 8760)), "1")
check("and so does Berlin", str(round_trips(Berlin, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 8760)), "1")

// The same sweep from the other side: how many wall clock readings in a year are not moments, and
// how many are two. A zone that moves twice loses one hour and gains another, so with a reading
// every half hour the answer is two of each — and the arithmetic of the year is that nothing else
// anywhere in it is anything but ordinary.
print("-- how much of a year is not a moment")
count_odd(z: ZoneId, from: LocalDate, days: int, want_twice: bool) -> int
    var found = 0

    for d in 0..<days
        for h in 0..<48
            var r = resolve(LocalDateTime(LocalDate(from.day + d), LocalTime(long(h) * us_per_minute * 30)), z)
            var odd = r match
                Skipped(gap, at, shifted) -> !want_twice
                Twice(earlier, later) -> want_twice
                _ -> false

            if odd then found += 1

    found
end count_odd

check("New York skips two half hours", str(count_odd(NewYork, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, false)), "2")
check("and repeats two", str(count_odd(NewYork, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, true)), "2")
check("Berlin the same", str(count_odd(Berlin, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, false)), "2")
check("and the same", str(count_odd(Berlin, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, true)), "2")
check("Kolkata skips none", str(count_odd(Kolkata, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, false)), "0")
check("and repeats none", str(count_odd(Kolkata, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, true)), "0")
check("UTC neither", str(count_odd(Utc, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, false) + count_odd(Utc, date_at(2026, 1, 1), 365, true)), "0")

// A zone is a table and a table has ends. Outside them every answer here is the zone's standard
// offset — right for a zone that has never moved, wrong for one that has — and a library that does
// not say so is a library that is wrong in silence. These checks assert the *limit*, not the
// correct answer, because the correct answer is not in the program.
print("-- the table has ends")
check("the span begins", instant_text(covered_from()), "2023-01-01 00:00 Z")
check("and ends", instant_text(covered_until()), "2028-01-01 00:00 Z")
check("July 2026, inside the span", str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(long(date_at(2026, 7, 1).day) * us_per_day)).minutes), "-240")
check("July 2022, before it, is answered as if the clocks never moved",
      str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(long(date_at(2022, 7, 1).day) * us_per_day)).minutes), "-300")
check("and July 2028, after it, the same way",
      str(offset_at(NewYork, Instant(long(date_at(2028, 7, 1).day) * us_per_day)).minutes), "-300")
check("a zone with no rules is right everywhere",
      str(offset_at(Kolkata, Instant(long(date_at(1815, 7, 1).day) * us_per_day)).minutes), "330")

// The awkward cases as a meeting rather than as a query — a rule whose reading lands in the hole
// every spring and in the overlap every autumn, which is what a weekly 02:30 or 01:30 appointment
// is. Nothing here is a special case in `expand`; it falls out of resolving each occurrence on its
// own instead of stepping the instant.
print("-- a meeting that lands where the clock does not")
var earlies: []Instant = [Instant(0); 3]
expand(Meeting(Sunday, time_at(2, 30, 0), NewYork), date_at(2026, 3, 1), earlies)
check("the Sunday before the change", zoned_text(to_local(earlies[0], NewYork)), "2026-03-01 02:30 -05:00")
check("the Sunday of it, pushed past the hole", zoned_text(to_local(earlies[1], NewYork)), "2026-03-08 03:30 -04:00")
check("and the Sunday after", zoned_text(to_local(earlies[2], NewYork)), "2026-03-15 02:30 -04:00")
// Pushing the skipped reading forward by the length of the hole lands it on the same instant an
// unmoved clock would have reached, so the week *into* the hole is an ordinary one and the week
// *out of* it is the short one. That is the opposite of where a reader expects the hour to go, and
// it is why this is asserted rather than reasoned about.
check("the week into the hole is a plain one", str(whole_hours(since(earlies[1], earlies[0]))), "168")
check("and the week out of it is an hour short", str(whole_hours(since(earlies[2], earlies[1]))), "167")

var lates: []Instant = [Instant(0); 3]
expand(Meeting(Sunday, time_at(1, 30, 0), NewYork), date_at(2026, 10, 25), lates)
check("the Sunday before the autumn change", zoned_text(to_local(lates[0], NewYork)), "2026-10-25 01:30 -04:00")
check("the Sunday of it, taken the first time round", zoned_text(to_local(lates[1], NewYork)), "2026-11-01 01:30 -04:00")
check("and the Sunday after", zoned_text(to_local(lates[2], NewYork)), "2026-11-08 01:30 -05:00")
check("the week containing the overlap is a plain one", str(whole_hours(since(lates[1], lates[0]))), "168")
check("and the one after it is an hour long", str(whole_hours(since(lates[2], lates[1]))), "169")

print("-- the edges of the arithmetic")
check("no room asked for, none used", str(expand(tuesdays, date_at(2026, 3, 3), [Instant(0); 0])), "0")
check("a duration runs backwards", str(whole_hours(since(mar3, by_calendar))), "-167")
check("and negating one says the same", str(whole_hours(-since(by_calendar, mar3))), "-167")
check("odd minutes of an hour and a half", str(odd_minutes(minutes(90))), "30")
// The two units below the second, which are the only lengths this representation can name and
// `seconds` cannot. Both rows are true of the same duration, which is the point of having them.
check("a length shorter than a second", str(whole_millis(millis(120))), "120")
check("and it is no seconds at all", str(whole_seconds(millis(120))), "0")
check("a reading with seconds in it", time_text(LocalTime(9 * us_per_hour + 7 * us_per_second)), "09:00:07")
check("and one with microseconds", time_text(LocalTime(9 * us_per_hour + 7 * us_per_second + 42)),
      "09:00:07.000042")
check("a meeting late enough to be tomorrow elsewhere",
      agenda_line(resolve_calendar(datetime_at(2026, 3, 3, 21, 0, 0), NewYork), NewYork, Kolkata),
      "2026-03-03 21:00 -05:00 / 2026-03-04 07:30 +05:30")
check("a wall reading before the epoch still resolves",
      instant_text(resolve_calendar(datetime_at(1969, 12, 31, 23, 30, 0), NewYork)), "1970-01-01 04:30 Z")
check("and reads back the same way",
      zoned_text(to_local(resolve_calendar(datetime_at(1969, 12, 31, 23, 30, 0), NewYork), NewYork)),
      "1969-12-31 23:30 -05:00")
check("the last day of a month that ends on the weekday asked for",
      date_text(last_weekday(2027, 10, Sunday)), "2027-10-31")
check("and one that does not", date_text(last_weekday(2026, 10, Sunday)), "2026-10-25")
check("the first of the month falling on the weekday asked for",
      date_text(nth_weekday(2026, 3, Sunday, 1)), "2026-03-01")
check("a zone is named", zone_name(NewYork) + " " + zone_name(Kolkata), "America/New_York Asia/Kolkata")
